<worksheet xml:id="ws-12">
     <title>Applications of Partial Derivatives</title>
   <exercise workspace="3.0in">
    <p>
      Find the plane tangent to <m>z=\ln(10x^2+2y^2+1)</m> at the point <m>P=(0,0,0)</m>
    </p>
   </exercise>

   <exercise workspace="3.0in">
    <p>
      Find a linear approximation for <m>f(x,y,z) = \sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}</m> at the point (3, 2, 6) to approximate <m>\sqrt{(3.20)^2+(1.97)^2+(5.99)^2}</m>.
    </p>
   </exercise>

   <exercise workspace="4.0in">
    <p>
      If a sound with frequency <m>f_s</m> is produced by a source traveling along a line with speed <m>v_s</m> and an observer is traveling along the same line but in the 
      opposite direction toward the source with velocity <m>v_o</m>, then the frequency of the sound heard by the observer is 
      <md>
        f_o = \left(\frac{332-v_o}{332-v_s}\right)f_s
      </md>
    This is known as the Doppler effect. Suppose that at a particular moment, you are in a train traveling at 34 meters per second and accelerating at 12 meters per 
    second squared. A train is approaching you from the opposite direction on the other track at 40 meters per second and accelerating at 1.4 meters per second squared and it 
    sounds its whistle, which has a frequency of 460 Hertz. At that instant, what is the perceived frequency that you hear and how fast is it changing?
    </p>
   </exercise>
</worksheet>